  "seoBody": "<h2>Can I use Jotform for inspection reports?</h2><p>You can use Jotform to collect inspection data, and it is very good at that. What it produces is a submission: a row of field values, or a PDF laid out as the form itself. For an internal record that is often enough.</p><p>It becomes a problem when the output has to go to a client, a regulator or head office, because what they expect is a report. That means your cover sheet, your standing wording on scope and methodology, observations written as prose rather than field values, photographs placed in the right section with captions, and a fault schedule with a job against each fault. Producing that from form submissions is a desk job that does not appear on any price list.</p><h2>How much does Jotform cost?</h2><p>The Starter plan is free, with 5 active forms and 100 submissions a month. Paid plans are Bronze at $39 a month, Silver at $49 and Gold at $129, each cheaper on annual billing. The paid tiers are single-user plans; Enterprise is the multi-user option. On sticker price Jotform is cheaper than Quickler and we will say so plainly.</p><h2>What does Quickler do that a form builder does not?</h2><p>It writes the report. Questions arrive in the WhatsApp or Telegram already on the worker's phone and they answer however suits: text, voice note, photo, document or a location pin. Voice notes are transcribed with your trade's vocabulary taught to the transcriber. When they say done, every message, photo and document from that walk is read together and written up into your own report template, with faults listed by location and each one raised as a job in the action plan.</p><h2>When should I just use Jotform?</h2><p>When what you need is structured data rather than a finished document. If the answers feed a spreadsheet or a dashboard and nobody is formatting a report afterwards, a form builder is the right tool and the cheaper one.</p>",
